<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Cat FAQ - Modern Product Design</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <header>
            <h1>Cat Facts FAQ</h1>
            <p>Everything you wanted to know about our feline friends.</p>
        </header>

        <section class="faq-list">
            <div class="example">
                <button class="faq-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq1">
                    <span>Why do cats purr?</span>
                    <svg class="chevron" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <path d="M5 7.5L10 12.5L15 7.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </button>
                <div id="faq1" class="details" hidden>
                    <div class="details-content">
                        <p>Cats purr for various reasons. While often a sign of contentment, they also purr to soothe themselves when stressed or in pain. Interestingly, the frequency of a cat's purr has been shown to improve bone density and promote healing!</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
                <button class="faq-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq2">
                    <span>Why do cats love cardboard boxes?</span>
                    <svg class="chevron" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <path d="M5 7.5L10 12.5L15 7.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </button>
                <div id="faq2" class="details" hidden>
                    <div class="details-content">
                        <p>Boxes provide cats with a sense of security and safety. In the wild, cats are both predators and prey, so having a confined space where they can't be snuck up on from behind or the side is instinctively comforting. It also helps them stay warm!</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
                <button class="faq-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq3">
                    <span>How much sleep do cats actually need?</span>
                    <svg class="chevron" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <path d="M5 7.5L10 12.5L15 7.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </button>
                <div id="faq3" class="details" hidden>
                    <div class="details-content">
                        <p>On average, cats sleep for 12 to 16 hours a day. Some can even sleep up to 20 hours! This is an evolutionary trait from their ancestors who needed to conserve energy for short, intense bursts of hunting.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
                <button class="faq-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq4">
                    <span>Why do cats bring "gifts" like mice?</span>
                    <svg class="chevron" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <path d="M5 7.5L10 12.5L15 7.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </button>
                <div id="faq4" class="details" hidden>
                    <div class="details-content">
                        <p>When your cat brings you a dead mouse or a toy, they are likely trying to teach you how to "hunt." In the wild, mother cats bring back prey to show their kittens how it's done. Since your cat views you as part of their family, they're just looking out for you!</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
                <button class="faq-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq5">
                    <span>Can cats see in total darkness?</span>
                    <svg class="chevron" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <path d="M5 7.5L10 12.5L15 7.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </button>
                <div id="faq5" class="details" hidden>
                    <div class="details-content">
                        <p>While they can't see in absolute pitch blackness, cats only need about one-sixth of the light humans do to see effectively. They have a special layer behind their retina called the tapetum lucidum, which reflects light back through the retina, increasing the light available to the photoreceptors.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
